 "annotation_import": {
  "_provenance": {
   "experiment": "Matched-initialization control for the crowd-sourced annotation (marginalized Dawid-Skene) benchmark",
   "date": "2026-07-14",
   "hypothesis": "Under the standard PPLBench protocol the three baselines (NumPyro/Stan/Turing) collapse to trials-as-chains ESS medians ~8 not because their NUTS kernels are slow, but because their default random init lets exactly one of four trials land in an inferior label-permutation mode (per-trial PLL ~-1850 vs the -1805..-1810 dominant band), which craters the pooled ESS. Impulse initializes deterministically at the uniform-simplex point (unconstrained z=0) and all its trials land in the dominant mode. Prediction: giving the baselines the SAME deterministic init removes the ~-1850 trials, recovers baseline ESS medians to thousands, and shrinks the n_eff/s gap vs Impulse to roughly the per-trial wall-time ratios (~2.2x / ~24x / ~56x).",
   "verdict": "CONFIRMED. With matched init, every baseline trial lands in the dominant mode (no PLL ~-1850 outlier remains). ESS medians recover from ~8 to ~18600-19000 (2000x-2300x). The residual Impulse/baseline n_eff/s gap collapses to the per-trial wall-time ratio: NumPyro 2.10x (wall 2.30x), Stan 23.2x (wall 24.9x), Turing 58.1x (wall 62x). This isolates the baseline collapse as an initialization-policy artifact, not a kernel-speed difference; Impulse's remaining advantage is exactly its per-iteration wall-clock (compiler) speed.",
   "init_point": {
    "description": "Deterministic uniform-simplex point, identical for every trial and every system; equals Impulse's NUTS start.",
    "constrained": {
     "prev": "ones(K)/K = [1/3, 1/3, 1/3]  (K=num_categories=3)",
     "confusion_matrix": "every one of k*K=30 rows = ones(K)/K = [1/3, 1/3, 1/3]  (k=10 labelers, K=3)"
    },
    "unconstrained": "z = 0 (62-dim: (K-1) + k*K*(K-1) = 2 + 60). Under the centered stick-breaking transform the uniform simplex is exactly the unconstrained origin, i.e. the symmetric point.",
    "impulse_evidence": "standard/crowd_sourced_annotation.jl build_constraint L111-119: init_prev = fill(1/K, K); init_conf = fill(1/K, k, K, K); fed as the constraint to ProbProg.generate in nuts_program (harness.jl L37) so the NUTS chain starts from that trace."
   },
   "mechanism_per_system": {
    "numpyro": "impl.matched_init_params(data) returns the constrained uniform dict; numpyro_harness.py converts it to unconstrained z via numpyro.infer.init_to_value (constrained_to_unconstrained) and passes init_params=z to mcmc.run for ALL trials (fixed across trials). The constrained sizes are NOT used to set inverse_mass_matrix (guarded), so the kernel is otherwise identical to the standard protocol. Verified: unconstrained z dim=62, max|z|=1.11e-16 (== symmetric point).",
    "stan": "impl.matched_init_params(data) returns the constrained uniform dict; stan_harness.py passes it as CmdStan `inits` (Stan inits are constrained) for every trial. Verified: Stan accepted the simplex init (no rejection); init printed.",
    "turing": "matched_init_params(data) returns NamedTuple (prev=fill(1/K,K), conf=fill(1/K,K,k*K)); turing_harness.jl passes it via DynamicPPL.InitFromParams (constrained, typed_identity) as sample()'s initial_params for every trial. Verified: logjoint at matched-init point finite (-3399.0 at k=10); init printed."
   },
   "config": "pplbench_configs/standard/crowd_sourced_annotation_matched_init.json (identical model block/seed 12345/iterations 4000/warmup 1000/trials 4 to the standard config; impulse arms omitted; matched_init:true added to the numpyro/stan/turing infer_args).",
   "commands": [
    "CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"\" ./run_pplbench.sh --device cpu --threads 32 --ppls numpyro standard/crowd_sourced_annotation_matched_init",
    "CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"\" ./run_pplbench.sh --device cpu --threads 32 --ppls stan standard/crowd_sourced_annotation_matched_init",
